From: TODO Your Name <todo@example.org>
Date: Sat, 30 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: ipq5332: add QPIC BAM and raw NAND
 controller nodes

The QPIC block at 0x079b0000 can drive either a serial (SPI) NAND chip
or a raw parallel NAND chip, depending on how the board is wired.
Upstream does not currently describe the serial flavour
("qcom,ipq5332-snand" bound to spi-qpic-snand) for this SoC at all, so
there is no pre-existing devicetree node at this address to reuse or
conflict with. Boards such as the Xiaomi BE3600 Pro (RN01) populate a
raw parallel NAND chip, which is driven by qcom_nandc.c through the
"qcom,ipq5332-nand" compatible; this adds that node standalone.

The clocks are the three QPIC clocks, named after the raw NAND
convention ("core", "aon", "io_macro"). qcom_nandc.c only requests
"core" and "aon"; "io_macro" is described for completeness and for a
future IO macro rate implementation.

Raw mode uses four QPIC BAM pipes: tx, rx, cmd and an additional status
channel. The QPIC block has its own dedicated BAM at 0x07984000,
separate from the shared BLSP1 BAM (blsp_dma) at 0x07884000 that the
other on-chip UART/SPI/I2C peripherals use -- confirmed against the
vendor device tree's real register/clock/interrupt values for this
board. That dedicated BAM is not yet described in upstream ipq5332.dtsi
at this kernel version either (the same "landed in a later point
release than 6.18.44" situation as the missing snand node above), so
this patch adds it as qpic_bam alongside the raw NAND node. Since
qpic_bam is its own independent BAM instance with its own pipe
numbering, pipes 0-3 here do not collide with anything -- they are
unrelated to blsp_dma's pipes 0-9 used by the shared-BAM peripherals
elsewhere in this file.

The NAND chip description (ECC strength/step size and bus width, all
matching the downstream device tree) is added as nandcs@0; the
partition table is board specific and lives in the board device tree.

Signed-off-by: TODO Your Name <todo@example.org>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/ipq5332.dtsi | 54 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 54 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/ipq5332.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/ipq5332.dtsi
@@ -687,6 +687,60 @@
 			};
 		};
 
+		qpic_bam: dma-controller@7984000 {
+			compatible = "qcom,bam-v1.7.4", "qcom,bam-v1.7.0";
+			reg = <0x07984000 0x1c000>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 66 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			clocks = <&gcc GCC_QPIC_AHB_CLK>;
+			clock-names = "bam_clk";
+			#dma-cells = <1>;
+			qcom,ee = <0>;
+			status = "disabled";
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * The QPIC block at 0x079b0000 can drive either a serial
+		 * (SPI) NAND chip or a raw parallel NAND chip, depending on
+		 * how the board is wired. Upstream does not currently
+		 * describe the serial flavour for this SoC, so this raw NAND
+		 * node is added standalone -- there is no pre-existing
+		 * devicetree node at this address to conflict with.
+		 */
+		qpic_nand_raw: nand@79b0000 {
+			compatible = "qcom,ipq5332-nand";
+			reg = <0x079b0000 0x10000>;
+			#address-cells = <1>;
+			#size-cells = <0>;
+
+			clocks = <&gcc GCC_QPIC_CLK>,
+				 <&gcc GCC_QPIC_AHB_CLK>,
+				 <&gcc GCC_QPIC_IO_MACRO_CLK>;
+			clock-names = "core",
+				      "aon",
+				      "io_macro";
+
+			dmas = <&qpic_bam 0>,
+			       <&qpic_bam 1>,
+			       <&qpic_bam 2>,
+			       <&qpic_bam 3>;
+			dma-names = "tx",
+				    "rx",
+				    "cmd",
+				    "sts";
+
+			status = "disabled";
+
+			nandcs@0 {
+				reg = <0>;
+				#address-cells = <1>;
+				#size-cells = <1>;
+
+				nand-ecc-strength = <4>;
+				nand-ecc-step-size = <512>;
+				nand-bus-width = <8>;
+			};
+		};
+
 		usb: usb@8af8800 {
 			compatible = "qcom,ipq5332-dwc3", "qcom,dwc3";
 			reg = <0x08af8800 0x400>;
